course: language
lesson: 49
title: A bit nippy and not ideal: British understatement
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Understatement is the opposite of hyperbole: it makes things sound SMALLER than they are. British and Irish people are famous for it: «Bit nippy out, innit» in a howling snowstorm, «Not bad at all» about a brilliant cake, «Slight problem in the kitchen, love!» while water floods the floor.
explain: People play things down to stay calm, to be polite, to avoid sounding big-headed, or to make a dry joke when something has gone wrong.
explain: Picture the family car with a flat tyre on the motorway at night, in the rain, and Dad saying «Well. This is not ideal». His words are small, but the danger is real: you stay in the car or behind the barrier and do what the grown-ups say.
explain: So with understatement, believe your eyes more than the small words: the snow, the score, the second slice of cake, the foam all over the table.
fact: Understatement means describing something as smaller, milder or less important than it really is, often to sound calm, polite or funny.
fact: «Bit nippy out, innit» on a snowy, windy January morning means «it's freezing»: coat, hat and gloves.
fact: «Not bad. Not bad at all» from many British grown-ups is warm praise; going back for a second slice shows what Dad really thinks.
fact: «This is not ideal» can mean «this is really bad»; on a motorway, a family waits behind the barrier, away from the traffic, and does what the grown-ups say.
fact: «A bit of a pickle» means a tricky problem, like being locked out at dusk with the keys inside; it has nothing to do with pickles.
fact: «Slight problem in the kitchen, love!» shouted over a burst pipe means a big problem: someone should come and help, and the water may need turning off at the stopcock (the tap that shuts off the house's water).
fact: Many British people praise by playing down, so as not to sound big-headed: «the girls did alright today» after a 5–0 cup final win means they were brilliant.
fact: In Scots, «dreich» means dull, grey and wet, «wee» means little and «the day» means today; «a wee bit dreich» in a storm means terrible weather, so the ferry is probably cancelled.
fact: «That went slightly less well than planned», said about foam all over the table, is a dry joke meaning it went very wrong.
fact: In Irish and British English, «it's not a bad day for it» often means a really lovely day.
fact: «I'm a bit peckish» means a little hungry, but people often say it to sound polite when they are quite hungry.
fact: Pilots speak calmly so that nobody panics: «a little bit of bumpiness» means strong shaking (turbulence) is coming, and it is safe if you stay belted in.
exercise: A snowy January morning in Yorkshire, the wind howling: «Bit nippy out, innit». What does it really mean? | It's freezing cold | It's a little bit cool | It's warm for January | It's going to be sunny
exercise: What should you wear after hearing «bit nippy» on that snowy morning? | A warm coat, a woolly hat and gloves | Only a T-shirt, because it's only «a bit» | Shorts, because it'll warm up later | Just a thin jumper over your shirt
exercise: Dad tastes Ava's first chocolate cake: «Not bad. Not bad at all». Then he takes a second slice. What does he think? | It's really good | It's just okay | It's a bit dry | It's too sweet for him
exercise: A flat tyre on the motorway, in the rain, at night: «Well. This is not ideal». What does it mean here? | It is a bad and a bit dangerous situation | It is a small thing, not worth worrying about | It is a fun adventure that you can play in | It is the best way to spend a rainy night
exercise: The family is locked out at dusk, keys inside: «We're in a bit of a pickle here». What is a pickle? | A tricky problem | A jar of onions | A small mistake by Dad | A kind of lock
exercise: Dad shouts upstairs: «Slight problem in the kitchen, love!» Water is pouring across the floor. What should happen? | Someone should come and help quickly | Nobody needs to come, it's only slight | Everyone should wait for it to dry by itself | Mum should finish her bath and then look
exercise: What is a stopcock? | The tap that turns off the house's water | A kind of rooster that wakes you up | A plug that stops the sink from draining | A clock that stops when water gets in
exercise: After a 5–0 cup final win, a British manager says «Yeah, the girls did alright today». What does she mean? | They played brilliantly | They played quite badly | They were just okay | They need more practice
exercise: Why do many British people praise by playing things down? | So as not to sound big-headed | Because they don't like praise | Because they can't think of big words | Because the law says they must
exercise: What does «dreich» mean in Scots? | Dull, grey and wet | Dry and very sunny | Cold and snowy | Windy but warm
exercise: A storm is bending trees on a Scottish island. A woman says «Aye, it's a wee bit dreich the day». What will probably happen to the ferry? | It will probably be cancelled | It will sail on time as usual | It will leave early to beat the rain | It will go faster in the wind
exercise: The experiment foams all over the table. Mr Hughes says «That went slightly less well than planned». How does he feel? | He's finding it funny | He's very, very angry | He thinks it nearly worked | He is going to cry
exercise: A perfect sunny Saturday at an Irish beach: «Ah sure, it's not a bad day for it». What does it mean? | It's a lovely day | It's just an okay day | It's a bad day | It's a rainy day
exercise: Mum's friend says «I'm a bit peckish» and then eats three plates of Sunday lunch. What did she mean? | Yes, I'm hungry, please | I just want one tiny crumb | I don't want lunch | I'm feeling sick
exercise: The pilot says «we may experience a little bit of bumpiness». What should you do? | Keep your seatbelt on and sit tight | Stand up and walk about, it's only a little | Go to the toilet before it starts | Unfasten your belt to be comfortable
exercise: What is understatement? | Making something sound smaller than it is | Making something sound bigger than it is | Saying the exact opposite of what you mean | Telling a lie to get out of trouble
test: «gran said it's a bit nippy so I'm going out in my t-shirt lol» There is snow on the ground. What is the problem? | «A bit nippy» was understatement; the snow shows it's freezing, so wrap up | Nothing, «a bit nippy» means a T-shirt is all you need on a snowy day like this one | The problem is that Gran should have said it was boiling hot outside | There is no problem, snow is warm if you keep moving about
test: Ava cries: «Dad only said NOT BAD about my cake!» Then she sees him eating his second slice. What should she think? | «Not bad at all» was big praise, and the second slice proves it | He hates the cake but is eating a second slice just to be polite | He thinks the cake is only just okay | He is still hungry because the slices were small
test: On the motorway hard shoulder at night, your little brother wants to get out «cos Dad said it's not ideal, so it's not that bad». What do you tell him? | «Not ideal» here means really bad; stay put and do what the grown-ups say | He is right, «not ideal» means everything is fine and safe, so he can get out | He can get out, but only to play on the grass for a bit | He should wait until the rain stops, then go for a walk
test: «Dad said there's a slight problem downstairs, so I'll finish my game first» You can hear water splashing. What is wrong? | «Slight» was playing it down: the splashing shows a big problem | Nothing, «slight» means the problem can wait until after your game | Nothing, Dad likes to fix things on his own | Splashing water is always a good sign in a kitchen
test: «that manager's well harsh, she only said 'alright' after they won 5-0» What have you missed? | She praised them the British way, by playing it down | She was really angry that they didn't score six | She thinks 5–0 is a bad result for a final | She didn't watch the match at all
test: ★ A storm, trees bending, and a Scot says «a wee bit dreich». A tourist from Spain thinks the ferry will sail. How could the tourist find out for sure? | Check the notice board at the ferry for the real news | Trust «a wee bit», because it means the storm is small | Wait on the ferry ramp in the rain until it comes | Ask the woman to say it again more slowly
test: ★ A cartoon teacher smiles calmly as foam covers the whole lab, saying «slightly less well than planned». What is the joke? | His calm small words don't match the big mess | He really thinks the experiment worked well | He doesn't know what foam looks like | Teachers are not allowed to be angry in front of a class
test: ★ Gran's friend says she's «a bit peckish», so you bring her one grape. Everyone else gets a full plate. What went wrong? | «A bit peckish» was a polite way of saying «I'm hungry» | Gran's friend asked for exactly one grape | «Peckish» means she only eats fruit | Nothing, one grape is a polite size for a guest at Sunday lunch
test: ★★ «the pilot said a little bit of bumpiness, so I can still go to the loo, yeah?» What is the safe answer? | No, he's calm on purpose; when the belt sign is on, stay seated and belted | Yes, a little bumpiness means it's fine to walk about the plane and use the loo | Yes, but only if you hold on to the seats as you go | No, because pilots always lie about the weather
test: ★★★ Same snowy morning. Gran: «bit nippy». The thermometer: minus 6. Your cousin: «it's like minus a million». Which tells you how cold it really is, and what has Gran done? | The thermometer; Gran played it down | Gran's words; the thermometer is wrong | Your cousin; Gran exaggerated | All three; nobody played anything down
test: ★★★ A manager says «alright» after a 5–0 win and «not ideal» after a 0–5 loss. What is the same about how she speaks? | She plays things down both times, the good and the bad | She exaggerates both times to make the fans happy | She means exactly what she says both times | She is being sarcastic both times
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the utterance «Bit nippy out», delivered upon a snow-covered morning with a gale blowing? | Understatement, the actual cold being severe | Hyperbole, the actual cold being negligible | A precise report of a mild temperature | A complaint concerning the regrettable absence of snow
exam: It is given that a parent, while water pours across the kitchen floor from a ruptured pipe, announces a «slight problem». Identify the appropriate response. | To attend promptly and assist, the problem being considerable | To disregard the announcement, the problem being slight | To await the drying of the floor | To complete one's present occupation before attending
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the Scots expression «a wee bit dreich the day», uttered during a severe storm. | That the weather today is extremely wet, grey and unpleasant | That the weather today is slightly sunny | That the weather tomorrow will be dry | That the storm has passed entirely
exam: A pilot announces that passengers «may experience a little bit of bumpiness». Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Considerable turbulence is expected; seat belts are to remain fastened | No turbulence of any consequence is to be expected | Passengers are invited to move about the cabin | The flight is to be diverted forthwith
